s bacterial superinfection (B1) but has minimal impact on other branches."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of systemic hypotension/tachycardia and fever contradict foreign body obstruction as the primary explanation for nasal symptoms?", "summary": "Systemic signs like hypotension and fever are less typical of foreign body obstruction (B2), providing weak contradiction."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the low-grade fever (37.5°C) and normal cardiopulmonary exam contradict systemic infection as the explanation for hemodynamic instability?", "summary": "Low-grade fever and normal exam weakly contradict systemic infection (B5) as the primary cause of instability."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.6], ["B1.1", 0.24], ["B1.2", 0.18]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.311], ["B1.2", 0.124], ["B2", 0.115]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.662], ["B1.1", 0.331], ["B1.2", 0.199]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No universal instability markers or time-critical syndrome patterns confirmed. Hypotension (90/48 mmHg) and tachycardia (124/min) with low-grade fever (37.5°C) suggest possible bacterial superinfection (B1) but do not meet severity criteria for immediate stabilization (e.g., no end-organ signs, airway compromise, or uncontrolled hemorrhage).", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Root posterior (0.65) and child branch posteriors (max 0.3308) do not meet confirmation threshold. No actionable_parent due to divergent management implications across bacterial superinfection subtypes. Info_exhaustion not triggered as untried discriminators (e.g., nasal swab cultures, imaging) remain available to resolve differential."}, "turn_budget_used": 3, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.92, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.3308823529411765, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Bleeding and infected vessel of Kiesselbach plexus"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Foreign body obstruction"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Nasopharyngeal carcinoma"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Septal perforation"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Sinusitis with bacterial superinfection"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Low-grade fever', 'Bloodstream infectious agent', 'Chronic infection', 'nasal deformity', 'deformity', 'Unexplained fevers', 'Inappropriate absence of fever'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "program_faults": [], "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.6}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1.1", "label": "Streptococcus pneumoniae/typical bacterial superinfection", "posterior": 0.331, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B1.2", "B2", "B1.3"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=0%, source=none]\n\n[RAG context for 'A 7-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department by his parents for worsening symptoms of previously diagnosed acute sinusitis' (score=0.68):]\n  Source: Clinical practice guideline (update): adult sinusitis. > History and Physical\n  Children show slight differences in the clinical signs of acute bacterial rhinosinusitis. Besides the 10-day duration, key symptoms, and double worsening, children are more prone to have fevers. Initi\n[RAG context for 'A 7-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department by his parents for worsening symptoms of previously diagnosed acute sinusitis' (score=0.76):]\n  Source: Fever in critically ill patients. > Differential Diagnosis\n  Nosocomial Sinusitis\n[RAG context for 'Initially' (score=0.73):]\n  Source: Medical malpractice: managing the risk. > Introduction\n  Foreign bodies are often a complication of lacerations and other soft tissue injuries.[2] Complications resulting from foreign bodies include inflammation, delayed healing, and damage to surrounding t\n[RAG context for 'Initially' (score=0.79):]\n  Source: Ultrasonography in Detection of Vaginal Foreign Bodies in Girls: A Retrospective Study. > Complications\n  It is important to note that foreign bodies are a potential cause of infection in all ages. Infection should be on the differential, especially in patients with systemic symptoms, including fever and \n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  worsening symptoms after initial treatment with decongestants → retained nasal foreign body with secondary infection → Foreign Body with Secondary Infection\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Perform nasal endoscopy to identify potential retained foreign body, which would explain refractory symptoms and unilateral onset\n  hypotension (90/48 mmHg) and tachycardia (124/min) → sepsis with hemodynamic compromise → Pseudomonas aeruginosa superinfection with hemodynamic compromise\n    (frequency: rare)\n    Suggestion: Check for Pseudomonas-specific markers in sinus cultures, especially if the patient has risk factors for opportunistic infections\n  progressive symptoms despite decongestant therapy → progressive bacterial superinfection with resistance to initial treatment → MRSA superinfection in community-acquired sinusitis\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Order nasal swab for MRSA screening and consider empiric MRSA-targeting antibiotics if clinical suspicion is high\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
